File: md-mode.info, Node: Installation

Installation
************

md-mode needs Emacs 29.1 or later and nothing else.  No pandoc, no
Node, no browser: rendering goes through `shr', the HTML engine that
ships with Emacs and drives `eww'.

Clone the repository and put it on your `load-path':

     git clone https://github.com/hayate/md-mode.git ~/.emacs.d/md-mode

     (add-to-list 'load-path "~/.emacs.d/md-mode")
     (autoload 'md-mode "md-mode" "Render the Markdown in this buffer." t)

With `use-package' on a recent Emacs:

     (use-package md-mode
       :vc (:url "https://github.com/hayate/md-mode" :rev :newest)
       :commands (md-mode md-split))

There is nothing to compile and no build step.  `md-mode' is
autoloaded, so nothing is read until you first run it.



File: md-mode.info, Node: Reading

Reading a document
******************

 -- Command: md-mode
     Render the Markdown in the current buffer, or return to the
     source.

     In a Markdown buffer, replace the window with the rendered
     document.  In a rendered document, switch back to the Markdown it
     came from.  Point is carried across in both directions, so you
     return to the passage you were reading.

The rendered document lives in its own read-only buffer.  The file on
disk is never touched by rendering, and cannot be saved in rendered
form.


Keys
====

`q'
     Back to the Markdown source.
`g'
     Re-render.
`s'
     Source and rendered document side by side, in step.  *Note Side
     by side::.
`RET'
     Follow the link at point.  *Note Navigating::.
`TAB'
     Fold or unfold the section at point.  Anywhere else, move to the
     next link.
`S-TAB'
     Cycle the folding of the whole document.
`n'
`p'
     Next and previous link.
`i'
     Jump to a heading with `imenu'.
`t'
     Jump to a heading chosen from a table of contents.
`l'
`r'
     Back and forward through documents you have followed.
`^'
     Open the directory the document lives in.


When it re-renders
==================

The view re-renders when you save the source, when you revert it, and
when the window width changes.  Folded sections stay folded across all
of these.

A rendered buffer holds one layout, so it cannot serve two windows of
different widths at once.  Showing a view in a second window leaves it
alone; press `g' in the window you want it sized for.



