File: md-mode.info, Node: File references

File references
===============

An inline code span holding something like `lib/thing.py:147' becomes
a link that opens the file at line 147.

It becomes a link *only if the file is actually there*.  An
unreachable reference stays ordinary inline code.  That makes
linkiness a live signal that the document and the code still agree:
move the document, or the file, and the link quietly stops being one.

A reference is resolved against, in order:

 1.  the document's own directory;  2.  the project root ofthat directory, found with `project-current' or by looking for a
     `.git';  3.  each entry of `md-link-roots'.

Set `md-link-roots' when a document points into a different checkout
than the one it lives in, which is the normal case for a folder of
specs:

     ;; .dir-locals.el in your specs directory
     ((nil . ((md-link-roots . ("~/src/api" "~/src/models")))))


What counts as a reference
--------------------------

The grammar is matched against a whole code span, never searched for inside
one.  Searching substrings finds references that were never written: the
`total' of `{"total": 42}', or the `12' of a timestamp like
`12:30'.

A candidate must have no whitespace, must not carry a URL scheme or a
Windows drive letter, and must look like a path -- that is, contain a
directory separator or end in a file extension.  A bare line number
such as `:513' is *not* resolved against a path mentioned earlier: the
order in which a renderer walks a paragraph is not the order in which
its author was thinking, and a confidently wrong link is worse than
none.

 -- Command: md-link-follow
     Follow the link at point.  A file reference opens the file at its
     line.  A link to a local Markdown document opens it rendered.
     Other local files open normally, and anything with a scheme goes
     to the browser.



File: md-mode.info, Node: Other documents

Other documents
===============

A link to a local Markdown file opens it rendered rather than in a
browser, so a folder of cross-referencing specs reads like a small
wiki.  Each document keeps its own view.

 -- Command: md-link-back
     Return to the document visited before this one.

 -- Command: md-link-forward
     Undo a `md-link-back'.

 -- Command: md-link-up
     Open the directory the document lives in.

A link to a local file that does not exist is reported rather than
handed to the browser, which would otherwise try to fetch
`./missing.md' as a URL.



File: md-mode.info, Node: Outline

Outline
=======

Headings in a rendered document are real outline headings, even though
the buffer contains no `#' markup: `shr' records the level of each
heading as a text property, and Emacs 29 added
`outline-search-function', which can search for exactly that.

`TAB' folds and unfolds a section, `S-TAB' cycles the whole document,
and `imenu' works.

 -- Command: md-outline-toc
     Jump to a heading chosen from the document's table of contents,
     indented by level.

Folds are overlays, and a re-render erases the buffer they live in.
Folded sections are therefore remembered by heading text and folded
again afterwards, so resizing the window does not unfold everything
you had put away.



File: md-mode.info, Node: Side by side

Side by side
============

 -- Command: md-split
     Show the Markdown source and the rendered document in two windows
     that follow each other.  Call it again to put the windows back.

The two sides are kept in step by *source line*, not by buffer
position.  This matters.  Rendering is lossy: a whole paragraph maps
to the line it started on, so mapping a position across and back does
not return where it began.  Round-tripping raw positions would make
point crawl backwards a little on every command.  An anchor moves only
when you move it, and both sides are set to the same value, so there
is nothing to drift.



File: md-mode.info, Node: Options

Options
*******


Rendering
=========

 -- User Option: md-render-max-width
     Maximum width, in characters, of a rendered line.  Default 100.
     Long lines are hard to read, so the text is capped even in a wide
     window.  Values below 20 are treated as 20.

 -- User Option: md-render-remote-images
     Whether to download and display images with remote URLs.  Default `nil'.
     *Note Safety::.

 -- User Option: md-render-highlight-code
     Whether to syntax-highlight fenced code blocks.  Default `t'.

 -- User Option: md-render-language-modes
     Map from a fenced-code language name to the major mode that
     highlights it.  An allowlist; *Note Safety::.

 -- User Option: md-render-max-highlight-size
     Fenced code blocks larger than this many characters are not
     highlighted.  Default 20000.

 -- User Option: md-render-max-image-size
     Image files larger than this many bytes are not displayed.
     Default 8388608.


Parsing
=======

 -- User Option: md-parse-front-matter
     Whether to render YAML front matter instead of hiding it.  Default
     `nil', which is what GitHub does.

 -- User Option: md-parse-html-blocks
     Whether to parse raw HTML blocks and splice them into the document.  Default
     `t'.


The view
========

 -- User Option: md-view-margin
     Columns of blank left margin.  Default 2; `0' disables it.

 -- User Option: md-view-hide-line-numbers
     Whether to hide line numbers in a rendered document.  Default
     `t'.  A rendered buffer's line numbers count rendered lines,
     which correspond to nothing the reader can use.

 -- User Option: md-auto-rerender-max-size
     Documents larger than this many characters are not re-rendered
     automatically.  Default 200000.  They still re-render with `g'.

 -- User Option: md-rerender-delay
     Seconds of idle time after a window resize before re-rendering.
     Default 0.3.


Navigation
==========

 -- User Option: md-link-roots
     Extra directories that file references may resolve inside.
     *Note File references::.

 -- User Option: md-link-max-references
     Most file references resolved in one document.  Default 500.

 -- User Option: md-link-follow-markdown
     Whether a link to a local Markdown file opens rendered.  Default
     `t'.

 -- User Option: md-outline-fold-on-render
     Whether folded sections stay folded when the document is
     re-rendered.  Default `t'.


Faces
=====

`md-code-block', `md-blockquote' and `md-blockquote-bar', plus
the standard `shr-h1' through `shr-h6', `shr-link' and
`shr-text'.



File: md-mode.info, Node: Safety

Safety
******

Markdown files arrive in cloned repositories, so a document is treated
as untrusted input.

 * Remote images are not fetched.  They render as a label.Opening a document should not make a network request to whoever
     wrote it.

 * URLs go through a scheme allowlist.  Anything else,`javascript:' and `data:' included, is dropped, and the label is
     left inert.  Control characters are stripped before the scheme is
     read, so an entity-encoded newline cannot smuggle one past.  This
     applies to Markdown links as well as to raw HTML.

 * A file reference must canonicalise to a path inside one ofits allowed roots, so a document cannot make a link out of
     `../../../../etc/passwd'.

 * Remote names such as `/ssh:host:/x.png' are refused beforeany filesystem call, and before project discovery.  Merely asking
     whether such a file is readable opens a connection.

 * Raw HTML goes through a tag allowlist.  `script', `style',`base', `object', `iframe', `svg', form and media tags are
     dropped before anything reaches the renderer.

 * A fenced code block's language selects a mode from`md-render-language-modes', an allowlist.  A mode name is never
     derived from a fence label, so a document cannot activate an
     arbitrary major mode and run its body.

 * Nesting depth, both block and inline, HTML node count,reference count and image size are all bounded.  The inline
     scanner precomputes delimiter matches and remembers failed
     searches, so a document full of unmatched brackets cannot freeze
     Emacs.

Local image paths are *not* confined to the document's directory.  A
document may point at `../assets/logo.png', which real repositories do
constantly.  Nothing leaves the machine, so the containment would cost
more than it buys.



File: md-mode.info, Node: Design

Design
******

     markdown text --md-parse--> DOM --shr--> rendered buffer

`md-parse.el'
     Markdown to DOM.  No UI dependencies; the tested unit.
`md-render.el'
     DOM to buffer, via `shr'.
`md-link.el'
     File references and links out of the document.
`md-outline.el'
     Folding, imenu, table of contents.
`md-mode.el'
     The command, the view mode, the split view and history.

`md-parse.el' produces the DOM representation `dom.el' uses and `shr'
consumes: `(TAG ATTRIBUTE-ALIST CHILD...)', with strings as text
nodes.  No HTML text is generated anywhere, so there is nothing to
escape and nothing to reparse.

`md-render.el' hands that tree to `shr' and overrides only the tags
whose defaults are wrong for a Markdown reader: `pre' gains syntax
highlighting, `blockquote' gains its bar, `img' resolves relative
paths and refuses the network, and lists lose the blank line `shr'
puts around a nested one.  Every other block tag is wrapped only to
record its source line.


Rendering is a transaction
==========================

A render erases and rebuilds the buffer.  Everything anchored to a
position in it -- point, window starts, folds, caches, imenu
markers -- would therefore be destroyed.

Rendering is consequently a transaction.  Before the buffer is
rebuilt, that state is captured as *source lines and heading names*,
which survive because they describe the document rather than the text
that happens to be displaying it.  Afterwards the state is resolved
against the new buffer and caches that pointed into the old one are
dropped.

Anything added to md-mode later should do the same: hold a source
line, not a position.



